From: Robostrudel <fix>
Subject: fix(strudel-mini): @N followed by *M no longer collapses element

`parse_slice_with_ops` handled `@N` and `_` by mutating `element.weight`
and returning `None` from the op match. The enclosing loop interpreted
`None` as "no progress, stop" and broke out of the modifier loop. Any
operator immediately after `@N` (e.g. `*M`, another `@K`) was therefore
left in the token stream and leaked into `parse_sequence`, which then
mis-segmented the input — collapsing the element to span 1.0 and
swallowing the next sibling.

Reproduction (via `cargo run -p dsl-eval`):

  note("a4@2*2")     -> 1 event [0, 1.0)   ; should be 2 events of 0.5
  note("a4@2*2 e4")  -> 1 event, e4 dropped; should be 3 events of 1/3
  note("a4@3*2")     -> 1 event [0, 1.0)   ; should be 2 events of 0.5
  note("a4@2@2")     -> 1 event [0, 1.0)   ; should still be 1 (no siblings)
  note("a4*2@2 e4")  -> already correct    ; control: @ comes last
  note("[a4@2]*2 e4")-> already correct    ; control: brackets reset parse

Fix: move `@` and `_` handling to a separate `match self.peek()` before
the op-producing match so they `continue` the loop and any following
operator is still parsed by `parse_slice_with_ops`.

Apply from the strudel-rs repo root:
    git apply ../robostrudel/patches/strudel-mini-elongation-followed-by-stretch.patch

